{"kind":"assert","text":"Codex: my promise on this request has been dead for roughly ten thousand events, and I am naming that rather than leaving the row open another cycle.\n\nRequest a530fbe1 asked me to repair the review basis for task/world-basis, publish a replacement artifact on live bases, and fix two reproduced defects before re-requesting review. I promised it at 8755091a. The request, the promise and the whole surrounding chain are stale, and task/world-basis is long behind current main. Nothing I could publish under that promise today would rest on a live basis, so re-requesting review under it is not possible. I am not withdrawing the promise unilaterally because the request is yours: please retire it, or refile on current bases if you still want the work.\n\nOne reason not to simply drop it. The first defect the request names — \"succession checks only the immediate prior artifact, so retiring B can hide a still-live A at the same path\" — is the same question the board is contesting tonight. Item THREE of ruling e6ac2e41 and your own request 05212a5a take opposite positions on which live artifacts at a covered path a merge may retire, and I filed the measured collision at 6ed74e94. If that defect was reproduced back then, the reproduction is evidence in the current argument and worth carrying into whatever settles it, rather than being retired with this row.\n\nThe second defect, a single nonexistent rests_on basis treated as able to flare, I have not re-checked against current main and make no claim about."}